  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Gusty Winds and Choppy Lakes Today\n\n-  Periodic Showers Through Tuesday, Limited Winter Impacts\n\n-  Mid-Week: High pressure, bringing valley inversions and fog\n\nRadar returns this afternoon indicate light showers in northwest\nMontana along the Idaho border and in the Idaho Panhandle.\nSatellite imagery depicts some moisture still streaming into the \nNorthern Rockies, supporting those showers in the northwest. The\nposition of the upper level jet, along with a series of weak\ndisturbances moving along the Canadian border, will keep isolated\nshowers and cloudy skies in the forecast for northwest Montana \nthrough Monday as daytime high temperatures stay a few degrees \nabove normal.\n\nA more pronounced disturbance will move into the Northern Rockies\non Tuesday, bringing more widespread precipitation. Temperatures\nstay on the high side, though, so chances for snow will be limited\nto elevations above about 6000 feet. High pressure will build on\nWednesday, resulting in strong valley temperature inversions and\npotential for morning fog along the more pronounced valleys of\nwestern Montana such as the Flathead, Mission, Missoula, and \nBitterroot valleys. As is typical with inversions, forecasting \ndaytime high temperatures becomes a challenge, so high\ntemperatures midweek and beyond may not reach their full potential\ndue to persistent stratus cloud cover and cold air pooling in the\nvalleys.\n\nLooking toward the end of the forecast period, ensemble guidance \nindicates the ridge axis migrates eastward. This will open the \ndoor to a more progressive southwest flow, reintroducing \nprecipitation chances and effectively scouring out any lingering \nvalley inversions.",
